<TextPage
  title="Returns & Refunds | Cone Sleeves UK"
  description="How to raise a problem with a printed packaging order, what we can put right, and what bespoke goods mean for returns."
  path={path}
  h1="Returns and Refunds"
  standfirst="Custom printed goods are made to your specification, which changes what a return means. Here is how problems are handled."
  updated="2026-08-13"
  crumbLabel="Returns & Refunds"
>
  <h2>Why Bespoke Goods Are Different</h2>
  <p>
    Everything we supply is made to a specification you have approved: your size, your
    board, your artwork, on a die cut to your own product. It cannot be resold to anyone
    else.
  </p>
  <p>
    That means there is no general right to return correctly produced goods because they
    are no longer wanted. It also means the stages before production — the specification,
    the flat sample and the proof — matter more than they would for a stock item, which is
    why we build them into every job.
  </p>

  <h2>When We Will Put Something Right</h2>
  <p>Tell us straight away if any of these apply:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The goods do not match the specification confirmed on your order.</li>
    <li>The goods do not match the proof you approved.</li>
    <li>There is a manufacturing or print defect — misregistration, banding, poor cutting.</li>
    <li>The quantity delivered falls outside the tolerance stated on your quotation.</li>
    <li>The goods arrived damaged in transit.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What We Cannot Put Right</h2>
  <p>These are worth stating plainly, because they are the common disappointments:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      An error that was present in the proof you approved. Approving a proof confirms the
      layout, content and spelling, so check it carefully rather than quickly.
    </li>
    <li>
      Colour that differs from what you saw on a screen. Screens emit light and board
      reflects it, and the two cannot match. Where colour is critical, ask about a printed
      sample before approving.
    </li>
    <li>
      A size that does not fit a product different from the one we sized against. If your
      cone or cup supplier changes, send us the new one before reordering.
    </li>
    <li>Ordering more than you turned out to need.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to Raise a Problem</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Tell us promptly.</strong> Transit damage in particular has to be reported
      quickly for a carrier claim to be possible.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Send photographs.</strong> Include the affected goods, the outer packaging if
      it is a transit issue, and any batch or label information on the box.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keep the goods.</strong> Do not dispose of them until we have agreed what
      happens, as they may need inspecting or returning.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tell us what would resolve it.</strong> A reprint, a credit or a partial
      credit are the usual outcomes, and knowing what you need helps us reach it faster.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What Happens Next</h2>
  <p>
    We look at the evidence against the approved proof and the order specification. Where
    the fault is ours, we agree a remedy with you — normally a reprint of the affected
    quantity, or a credit where a reprint would not arrive in time to be useful.
  </p>
  <p class="pending">
    <strong>Timescales and process detail pending</strong>
    The period within which problems must be reported, the return process, and how credits
    or refunds are issued have not been supplied for this site. Rather than invent figures,
    they are left blank. These must be confirmed and published here before launch, and they
    should match what is set out in the <a href="/terms-and-conditions/">terms and conditions</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Cancelling an Order</h2>
  <p>
    Because production is bespoke, an order can normally be changed or cancelled without
    cost only before production begins. Once a die has been made or the job is on press,
    costs have already been incurred. Tell us as early as possible and we will be
    straightforward about what is and is not recoverable.
  </p>

  <h2>Talk to Us</h2>
  <p>
    Most problems are resolved faster by a conversation than by an email chain. Call
    <a href={TEL_HREF}>{CONTACT.phoneDisplay}</a> or email
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.email}`}>{CONTACT.email}</a> with your order reference.
  </p>
</TextPage>
